<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lahaina</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lahaina is a town on the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>. Long a royal centre of Maui's chiefs, it served as the capital of the Kingdom of Hawaii from about 1820 to 1845, and in the mid-19th century it was a major port of the Pacific whaling fleet and a centre of missionary activity. Much of the historic town was destroyed by a wildfire in August 2023.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lahaina.jpg" alt="Lahaina" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Calbear22 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%C4%81hain%C4%81_oceanfront.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lahaina lies on the north-west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a>. It was originally called Lele in Hawaiian and was known for its breadfruit trees; the name Lahaina is generally explained as a combination of the words for sun and cruel, with several other proposed meanings. The town lies in the rain shadow of the West Maui Mountains and is one of the driest places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>. According to Hawaiian tradition, the first ruler of western Maui was Haho, and the line that followed him produced later rulers, among them Piʻilani, the first to rule the whole island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1738 Lahaina and most of West Maui saw a series of battles between rival chiefs of the royal family and their allies from Hawaii Island and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a>. When George Vancouver visited in 1793, he described a village among breadfruit and coconut groves whose people were impoverished by long wars. From 1802 to 1803 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a> stationed his fleet of war canoes there. American Protestant missionaries set up the first mission station on Maui at Lahaina in 1823, and in 1831 they founded Lahainaluna Seminary, where Hawaiian students, among them the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-malo">David Malo</a>, were taught religion and crafts. Its press issued the first newspaper in the Hawaiian language in 1834, and teachers and students helped translate the Bible into Hawaiian.</p><p>In the 19th century Lahaina was an important stop for whalers, who came for fresh water, fruit and vegetables. Conflict with the missionaries sometimes led to riots, and in 1827 the British whaler John Palmer shelled the town. Governor Hoapili built the Old Lahaina Fort in 1831. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii">Kamehameha III</a> lived in a royal compound on the island of Mokuʻula from 1837 to 1845, and on 8 October 1840 he proclaimed the first Hawaiian constitution at Luaʻehu in Lahaina. In 1845 he moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a>, which was growing in commercial importance. A fire on 1 January 1919 destroyed more than thirty buildings and led to the creation of the Maui Fire Department.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lahaina is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>-designated place in Maui County, with a population of 12,702 at the 2020 census. Its historic district, centred on Front Street, held 60 historic sites, among them the Old Lahaina Courthouse, the fort and the Baldwin House. A banyan tree planted in 1873 to mark the 50th anniversary of the missionaries' arrival became the largest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Wildfires in August 2023 destroyed about 80 per cent of the town.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lahaina, Hawaii” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1819 American whaling ships called for provisions, and in the peak years of the 1840s and 1850s hundreds a year put in at Honolulu and <b>Lahaina</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hawaii">Kingdom of Hawaii · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Lahaina</b> later served as capital of the Kingdom of Hawaii.</blockquote><a href="/state/chiefdom-of-maui">Chiefdom of Maui</a></li></ul></section>
